{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>If he has not put his hand to his fellow's property.</b> The reference is to a deposit that was given into his hand, and so too is the view of the Targum: במה דמסר ליה חבריה — 'with what his fellow gave him.' He rendered the deposit in the language of מלאכה, for a person's property is called מלאכה, as it is written (Genesis 33:14): לרגל המלאכה — and he said this concerning the livestock. The meaning is that it is forbidden to make use of a deposit at all; if the deposit was a living creature, it is forbidden to work with it; and if the deposit was utensils, it is forbidden to use them, for use of utensils is also called מלאכה, since utensils are made for work, for use. Our Rabbis of blessed memory were precise in the Gemara in Bava Metzi'a at the end of chapter HaMafqid, and said of it: 'If he put his hand, he is liable' — that is, if he made any use of it at all and worked with it in any way whatsoever, then even if its value did not diminish he is liable. And there Rav and Levi disagree: one said שליחות יד (unauthorized use) requires diminution, and one said it does not require diminution. And they concluded there in the Gemara that it does not require diminution, and he may not make use of it at all, even for a use that causes no diminution; rather, it is forbidden to make use of it at all."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:58.566861286Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}